    "notes": "MODEL: owner-FCF DCF (component route) with the cyclical adaptations -- Zebra is a US operating company selling rugged enterprise hardware (mobile computers, scanners, printers, RFID, machine vision) whose demand follows enterprise capex/refresh cycles (FY22->FY23 revenue -21%, owner FCF negative in FY23). Base case therefore glides margins back to a mid-cycle adjusted-EBITDA margin (22.5%) rather than capitalising the FY26 up-cycle peak (23.5-24.0% guided). FY0 is FY2026E built from the Q2-26 raised guidance (sales +14-16% incl. Elo Touch, acquired Q4-25 for ~$1.3B; adj. EBITDA margin 23.5-24.0%; FCF >$1.0B); base_revenue = FY25 $5,396M x 1.15. Year 1 = FY2027. Net debt is the 2026-07-04 balance sheet (debt $2,776M incl. $2,275M now current -- a refinancing event, not a solvency one at ~2x EBITDA; cash $157M); H2-26 FCF is assumed to be consumed by the ongoing buyback, so no cash build is credited. last_fcf = guided FY26 FCF $1,000M - SBC $190M (levered, after ~$140M interest); the engine itself builds UNLEVERED owner FCF from EBITDA and deducts net debt once in the bridge, so interest is not double-counted. The ~$73M IEEPA tariff refund claim is excluded (contingent, one-off). Interest income is 0: the company is net-debt. EBITDA basis: adjusted EBITDA (pre-SBC); SBC charged as its own line.",
